\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces BDNK characteristic speeds vs.\ temperature for (a) the quark-gluon plasma and (b) neutron star matter. The sound speed $c_s/c$ (blue/pink) and the maximum BDNK dissipative characteristic speed (green) are both bounded by $c$ (gray dotted line). The Eckart/Landau theory predicts acausal infinite-speed dissipative propagation (orange dashed). The QCD crossover at $T_c \approx 155$\,MeV and the deconfinement transition region are indicated.}}{15}{figure.1.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces (a)~Allowed region in the dimensionless BDNK frame-coefficient plane $(a_E, a_\beta )$ for three values of $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2/c^2$: conformal fluid ($1/3$, green), intermediate nuclear matter ($1/2$, orange), and stiff nuclear EOS ($0.7$, red). The shaded regions are the causal, stable, strongly hyperbolic parameter space. Specific theoretical predictions are marked: holographic (AdS/CFT), kinetic theory (BGK), and the numerical choice of Pandya et~al.\ (2022). (b)~Transport coefficient space $(\eta _{\mathrm {s}}/s,\, \zeta /s)$ showing the KSS bound $\eta _{\mathrm {s}}/s = 1/(4\pi )$ and estimated ranges for the quark-gluon plasma, neutron star cores, and binary merger remnants.}}{17}{figure.1.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Relativistic Alfv\'en speed $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c$ (colored solid) and fast magnetosonic speed $v_{\mathrm {f}}/c$ (green dash-dotted) versus magnetic field strength $B$ for (a) neutron star, (b) magnetar, and (c) relativistic jet environments. The classical Alfv\'en speed (gray dashed) can exceed $c$; the relativistic expression automatically saturates below $c$ (gray dotted). Sound speed $c_s/c$ is shown for reference (blue dashed).}}{31}{figure.2.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Full 7-wave characteristic structure of relativistic MHD as a function of the angle $\theta _{Bk}$ between the wavevector and the magnetic field, for three astrophysical regimes: (a)~pulsar ($B = 10^{12}$\,G, $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c \approx 0.005$), (b)~magnetar surface ($B = 10^{15}$\,G, $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c \approx 0.45$), (c)~magnetar interior ($B = 10^{16}$\,G, $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c \approx 0.8$). Forward-propagating waves are shown at full opacity; backward waves at reduced opacity. In all cases, every characteristic speed remains strictly below $c$ (red shaded region), in contrast to the classical fast magnetosonic speed (dotted) which can exceed $c$ for strong fields. The entropy wave has zero speed in the fluid frame.}}{32}{figure.2.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Squared sound speed $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2/c^2$ as a function of energy density (in units of nuclear saturation energy density $\varepsilon _{\mathrm {nuc}} \approx 150$\,MeV/fm$^3$) for three representative equations of state: ideal relativistic gas (blue), quark-gluon plasma (brown), and nuclear matter (pink). The causality bound $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2 = c^2$ (red line) and the conformal limit $c_{\mathrm {s}}^2/c^2 = 1/3$ (gray dotted) are shown. The QCD crossover produces a pronounced dip in the QGP sound speed near $\varepsilon \approx \varepsilon _{\mathrm {nuc}}$.}}{44}{figure.2.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces BDNK transport coefficients as functions of baryon density $n_B/n_0$ (where $n_0 = 0.16$\,fm$^{-3}$ is nuclear saturation density) at $T = 10^9$\,K for three nuclear EOSs: APR (red solid), SLy (blue dashed), and DD2 (green dash-dotted). (a)~Shear viscosity $\eta $: dominated by nn-scattering, sensitive to the effective mass. (b)~Bulk viscosity $\zeta $: shows dramatic enhancement at the direct Urca threshold (highest density peak in DD2). (c)~Thermal conductivity $\kappa $: governed by electron conduction, sensitive to the proton fraction. The vertical dotted line marks nuclear saturation density.}}{46}{figure.2.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The relativistic compactness parameter $\xi =p_{0}/(\varepsilon _{0}c^{2})$ across astrophysical regimes. Relativistic corrections to the critical Rayleigh number exceed 1\% when $\xi >0.01$ (dashed red line) and become of order unity when $\xi \sim 1/3$ (dot-dashed line).}}{57}{figure.3.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces (a)~Thermal signal speed $v_{\mathrm {sig}}/c$ as a function of temperature for QGP: the Eckart and Landau--Lifshitz formalisms are acausal ($v_{\mathrm {sig}} > c$), while Israel--Stewart and BDNK respect causality. (b)~Allowed region in the BDNK frame-coefficient space $(a_E, a_\beta )$ at $T = 200$\,MeV, with three physical choices marked.}}{59}{figure.3.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces (a)~Critical Rayleigh number $R_{c}^{\mathrm {rel}}$ vs.\ $\xi $ for three boundary types. (b)~$\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ contours in NS ocean parameter space ($\xi =0.015$).}}{75}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces (a)~BDNK and IS thermal relaxation length scales as functions of temperature for NS ocean parameters ($\rho = 10^9\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {g\,cm^{-3}}$). Both scales are far below the inter-particle spacing. (b)~Ratio $\ell _{\mathrm {IS}}/\ell _{\mathrm {BDNK}}$ and the $\mathcal {O}(\ell /\lambda )^2$ correction for a macroscopic scale $\lambda = 10$\,m.}}{77}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces (a)~Classical growth rate vs.\ wavenumber at several $\mathrm {Ra}/\mathrm {Ra}_c$. (b)~BDNK (3 modes) vs.\ IS (5 modes): dashed curves show IS relaxation modes absent in BDNK.}}{87}{figure.4.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Full BDNK dispersion relation for NS crust parameters. (a)~$|\mathrm {Re}(\omega )|$ vs.~$k$: two sound modes follow $c_s k$ at low $k$. (b)~$|\mathrm {Im}(\omega )|$ vs.~$k$: damping rates follow $\kappa _T k^2$ (thermal) and $\Gamma _s k^2$ (sound). (c)~Mode trajectories in the complex $\omega $ plane. (d)~Numerical values at $k_{\mathrm {crit}}$.}}{89}{figure.4.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces (a)~Rayleigh quotient ratio (green) and critical Ra ratio (red) vs.\ $\xi $. (b)~Energy balance at marginal stability: viscous dissipation, buoyancy release, and entropy production.}}{96}{figure.4.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces (a)~Critical Rayleigh numbers for 4 regimes and 3 boundary types. (b)~Marginal stability curves $R_{c}(a)$ showing upward shift with $\xi $.}}{102}{figure.4.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces (a)~Ratio $R_c^{(\mathrm {rel})}/R_c^{(\mathrm {class})} = 1 + \Xi $ for six astrophysical systems. (b)~Compactness parameter $\Xi $ on a logarithmic scale; the dashed line marks the 1\% correction threshold.}}{103}{figure.4.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces (a)~Pattern selection phase diagram: rolls stable at low $gd/c^{2}$ and $c_s^{2}/c^{2}$; hexagons preferred at larger values. (b)~Landau coefficients vs.\ $\xi $ at $gd/c^{2}=0.01$.}}{109}{figure.4.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces (a)~Astrophysical parameter space in the ($\xi $, Ra) plane. Shaded rectangles show estimated ranges for various systems. Black dashed lines mark the critical Rayleigh number (both-free and both-rigid boundaries) as a function of $\xi $. The blue-shaded region below the curves is stable. (b)~Observable signatures of relativistic convection: convective heat flux suppression and velocity reduction as functions of $\xi $ (at fixed supercriticality $R/R_c=2$).}}{116}{figure.4.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Proto-neutron star neutrino luminosity $L_\nu (t)$. \emph {Left:} Comparison of inviscid, Israel--Stewart, and BDNK predictions for a $1.4\,M_\odot $, $R = 12$~km proto-NS with $T_{\rm core} = 50$~MeV. The IS model shows characteristic ringing at $t \lesssim 0.6$~s (shaded region) that is absent in the BDNK signal. \emph {Right:} Fractional deviation from the inviscid baseline, highlighting the IS artifact and the smooth BDNK correction.}}{120}{figure.4.10}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Frame-dragging effect on inertial waves in rotating neutron stars. \textbf {(a)}~Inertial-wave frequency $|\sigma | = 2\Omega _{\mathrm {eff}}\cos \vartheta $ vs.\ wavevector angle~$\vartheta $ for a millisecond pulsar ($\Omega = 4498$~rad\,s$^{-1}$) at several compactness values. Frame dragging reduces $\Omega _{\mathrm {eff}}$ and hence the wave frequency. \textbf {(b)}~Percentage shifts in frequency (frame-dragging) and damping rate (enthalpy correction) as functions of stellar compactness $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$. For a canonical neutron star with $\mathcal {C} = 0.2$, the corrections are of order 5--30\%.}}{130}{figure.5.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Stability diagram for rotating convection in millisecond pulsars. The critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ is plotted against the physical Taylor number $\mathrm {Ta}$ for several values of the enthalpy ratio $w/(\rho _0c^{2})$. The shaded band marks the regime $\mathrm {Ta} \sim 10^{12}\text {--}10^{18}$ relevant to millisecond pulsars. Solid curves: stationary convection; thin curves: overstability ($\mathrm {Pr} = 10^{-3}$). The relativistic enhancement of $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ shifts the stability boundary upward, strengthening rotational stabilisation. The top axis shows the corresponding rotation rate for $\nu = 10$~cm$^{2}$\,s$^{-1}$ and $d = 100$~m.}}{141}{figure.5.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_c(\mathrm {Ta})$ for rotating convection with realistic millisecond pulsar parameters. Curves are shown for Newtonian ($\xi = 1$) and three nuclear EOS models: APR ($\xi = 1.15$), SLy ($\xi = 1.28$), and a stiff EOS ($\xi = 1.45$). Markers indicate specific transport coefficient values for PSR~J1748$-$2446ad ($f_{\rm spin} = 716$~Hz, $d = 100$~m); circles: $\nu = 1$, squares: $\nu = 10$, diamonds: $\nu = 100$~cm$^2$\,s$^{-1}$. The shaded band spans the plausible MSP parameter range. The relativistic correction is $\sim 39\%$ for SLy at the reference parameters.}}{143}{figure.5.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Overstability in neutron star cores. \textbf {(a)}~Growth rate of the overstable mode near onset for classical (Navier--Stokes), BDNK first-order, and Israel--Stewart formulations, at $\mathrm {Pr} = 10^{-3}$, $T_{1} = 10^{4}$. The BDNK curve is shifted downward by the enthalpy factor $\rho _0c^{2}/w$; the IS curve includes a small correction from $\tau _{q}\omega \neq 0$. \textbf {(b)}~Oscillation frequency of the overstable mode vs.\ relativistic Taylor number for several enthalpy ratios. The frequency follows $\omega \propto \mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}^{1/2}$ (inertial scaling) but is suppressed by $(\rho _0c^{2}/w)^{1/2}$ in the relativistic theory.}}{153}{figure.5.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Mode structure comparison: BDNK cubic vs.\ Israel--Stewart quintic for overstability in a neutron star core ($\mathrm {Pr} = 7.8\times 10^{-5}$, $T_{1,\rm rel} = 6.6\times 10^{19}$). \textbf {(a)}~Growth rates $\mathrm {Re}(\sigma )$ vs.\ Rayleigh number. BDNK (solid green, 3 modes) and IS physical modes (dashed orange) are nearly identical. The two IS relaxation modes (not shown at this scale) have $\mathrm {Re}(\sigma ) \sim -10^{19}$. \textbf {(b)}~Oscillation frequencies $|\mathrm {Im}(\sigma )|$. The physical modes oscillate at hydrodynamic frequencies ($\lesssim 10$), while the IS relaxation modes (dotted horizontal lines) oscillate at $\sim 1/\hat \tau _{q,\pi } \sim 10^{19}$--$10^{20}$, well outside the hydrodynamic regime.}}{156}{figure.5.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Double-diffusive convection in proto-neutron stars. \textbf {(a)}~Stability regions in the (Lewis number, density ratio) plane for nuclear matter with $\mathrm {Pr} = 10^{-2}$. The shaded band marks the proto-NS neutrino regime ($\mathrm {Le} \sim 10$--100). The arrow indicates the upward shift of the oscillatory boundary due to relativistic enthalpy corrections. \textbf {(b)}~Critical Rayleigh number vs.\ Taylor number for several values of the density ratio $R_{\rho }$, showing how the stabilising lepton gradient raises the convective threshold. The proto-NS regime is highlighted.}}{168}{figure.5.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces R-mode instability window in the $(\log _{10} T, f_{\rm spin})$ plane for a $1.4\,M_\odot $, $R = 12$~km neutron star. The instability region lies above each curve. Green (BDNK): narrower window due to unsuppressed bulk viscosity. Orange dashed (IS): wider window due to relaxation suppression of $\zeta $ at high $T$. Blue (Navier--Stokes): intermediate. Purple circles: millisecond pulsars; pink squares: LMXBs. The BDNK window is consistent with most observed pulsars being stable.}}{171}{figure.5.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Relativistic Alfv\'en speed $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c$ as a function of magnetic field strength $B$ for four representative neutron star densities. Solid lines: relativistic formula~\eqref {eq:rel4-24}; dashed lines: classical formula $V_A = B/\sqrt {4\pi \rho }$. The horizontal dotted line marks the causal limit $v_{\mathrm {A}}= c$. The relativistic saturation becomes significant for $B \gtrsim 10^{15}$~G in the outer core and for $B \gtrsim 10^{16}$~G in the inner core.}}{183}{figure.6.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Stability diagram for magneto-convection: critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ versus Chandrasekhar number $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$. The classical curve (solid blue) is compared with relativistic curves at $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2} = 0.01$, $0.05$, and $0.1$ representative of neutron star outer core, core, and magnetar interior respectively. The shaded region marks the magnetar parameter regime. The $\pi ^{2}Q$ asymptote (dotted grey) is approached for all boundary conditions at large $Q$.}}{194}{figure.6.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {(a)}~Stability phase diagram for magnetar magneto-convection: critical temperature gradient $\beta _c$ versus magnetic field strength~$B$. The relativistic curve (SLy, $\xi = 1.28$) lies below the Newtonian curve, indicating that convection onset occurs at lower temperature gradients when relativistic inertia is included. Horizontal lines mark typical proto-NS ($\beta \sim 0.1$~K/cm) and mature NS ($\beta \sim 10^{-5}$~K/cm) gradients. \textbf {(b)}~Critical Rayleigh number versus $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ with magnetar parameter values (circles). The $\pi ^2 Q$ asymptote (dotted) is approached at large~$Q$.}}{196}{figure.6.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Numerical verification of the $\pi ^{2}Q$ law. \emph {Left:} Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ versus Chandrasekhar number $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ for free boundaries (solid blue), rigid boundaries (dashed purple), and relativistic free boundaries with $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2} = 0.05$ (dash-dotted red). \emph {Right:} The ratio $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}/Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ converges to $\pi ^{2}$ (dotted grey line) for all boundary conditions as $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}\to \infty $, confirming the universality of the $\pi ^{2}Q$ law in the relativistic theory.}}{204}{figure.6.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Oblique magnetic field and thermal convection in magnetar interiors. \emph {Left:} Effective Chandrasekhar number (normalised to the aligned-field classical value) versus the angle $\vartheta $ between $\mathbf {B}$ and $\mathbf {g}$, for several values of $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2}$. \emph {Right:} Overstable frequency shift $\sigma _{\mathrm {rel}}/\sigma _{\mathrm {class}}$ versus $v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2}$ at $\vartheta = 45^{\circ }$.}}{213}{figure.6.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Magnetar torsional oscillation modes versus SGR~1806$-$20 QPOs. \emph {Left:} Computed magneto-elastic mode frequencies (coloured ticks) for angular degrees $\ell = 2, 4, 7, 10, 13$, overlaid with observed QPO frequencies (red vertical bands). The low-frequency QPOs (18--30~Hz) require $\ell \geq 7$ modes or core-coupled Alfv\'en continuum oscillations. \emph {Right:} Quality factor $Q$ of $\ell = 2$ modes computed with BDNK (green circles) and IS (orange squares) damping. BDNK predicts systematically lower $Q$, especially at high frequencies where IS relaxation artificially reduces the damping.}}{214}{figure.6.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Magneto-inertial wave dispersion in rotating neutron stars. \emph {Left:} Frequency $\omega $ versus wavenumber $k$ for the two transverse magneto-inertial branches at four values of $v_{\mathrm {A}}/c$. The light line $\omega = kc$ (dotted grey) is never crossed, confirming causality. \emph {Right:} Phase velocity $v_{\mathrm {ph}}/c$ versus $k$, showing that all modes remain sub-luminal.}}{224}{figure.7.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional stability surface for rotating magnetar convection: $\log _{10}\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}_{c}$ as a function of $\log _{10}\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ and $\log _{10}Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ (two free boundaries). Warmer colours indicate higher critical Rayleigh numbers. The competition between rotation and magnetic field is visible as a valley along the $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$ axis at moderate $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$.}}{233}{figure.8.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Combined rotation and magnetic field stability for a proto-neutron star ($M = 1.6\,M_\odot $, $R = 20$~km, $f_{\rm spin} = 100$~Hz, $B = 10^{13}$~G). \textbf {(a)}~Contour plot of $\mathrm {Ra}_{\mathrm {rel}}^{(c)}(\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}, Q_{\mathrm {rel}})$ for two free boundaries. The proto-NS location (star) lies well above the stability surface. The dashed white line indicates the approximate overstable/stationary boundary. \textbf {(b)}~1D slice at $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}= 2.2\times 10^{18}$: stationary (solid blue) and overstable (dashed orange) critical Rayleigh numbers versus $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}$. The horizontal green line marks the actual Ra from neutrino-driven convection. Stationary convection is preferred and the proto-NS is vigorously convective.}}{236}{figure.8.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces Magnitude of relativistic corrections across astrophysical systems. \emph {Top:} Individual correction parameters: magnetic ($v_{\mathrm {A}}^{2}/c^{2}$, red), rotational ($\Omega ^{2}d^{2}/c^{2}$, blue), and thermal ($p/(\rho c^{2})$, green). \emph {Bottom:} Leading-order fractional correction to the critical Rayleigh number. Blue bars: negligible ($<10^{-6}$); orange: small ($10^{-6}$--$10^{-2}$); red: significant ($>10^{-2}$). Only in neutron star cores and magnetar interiors do the relativistic corrections reach the percent level.}}{244}{figure.8.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces Gravitational wave characteristic strain from proto-NS convection at 10~kpc. \emph {Left:} $h_c(f)$ for inviscid (blue) and BDNK (green) models, overlaid with aLIGO, ET, and CE sensitivity curves. The signal peaks near the convective frequency $f_{\rm conv} \approx 1250$~Hz. The BDNK signal is reduced by $\sim 20\%$ due to enhanced viscous dissipation. \emph {Right:} Ratio $h_c^{\rm BDNK}/h_c^{\rm inviscid}$, showing that the BDNK suppression increases at high frequencies where the lower dissipation scale steepens the spectral cutoff.}}{247}{figure.8.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.5}{\ignorespaces TOV equilibrium profiles for the Schwarzschild interior (uniform energy density) solution at four compactness values $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$. \emph {Left:} Energy density (constant) and enthalpy density $(\varepsilon +p)/\varepsilon _{c}$. \emph {Centre:} Pressure profile $p(r)/\varepsilon _{c}$. \emph {Right:} Metric potential $\Phi (r)/c^{2}$. The steepening of $p(r)$ and deepening of the potential well with increasing compactness control the relativistic corrections to convective stability (cf.\ \S \,\ref {subsec:rel-56-summary}).}}{255}{figure.8.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.6}{\ignorespaces TOV profiles for three nuclear EOSs (APR, SLy, BSk21), solved numerically with polytropic approximations. \emph {Left:} Normalised energy density $\varepsilon (r)/\varepsilon _{c}$. Unlike the uniform-density (Schwarzschild interior) model, realistic EOSs produce monotonically decreasing $\varepsilon (r)$. \emph {Centre:} Normalised pressure $p(r)/p_{c}$. Stiffer EOSs (APR) have more slowly declining pressure profiles. \emph {Right:} Pressure-to-energy ratio $\xi (r) = p(r)/\varepsilon (r)$, which controls the enthalpy--inertia factor $\mathcal {I} = 1 + \xi $ in the perturbation equations. Dotted gray lines show the Schwarzschild interior comparison. The central value $\xi _{c} \sim 0.1$--$0.3$ quantifies the relativistic enhancement of buoyancy driving.}}{256}{figure.8.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c,\mathrm {rel}}$ vs.\ compactness for $l=1$, 2, 3 modes (free boundary, uniform-density sphere). Dotted lines mark the Newtonian values. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ra}_{c,\mathrm {rel}} / \mathrm {Ra}_{c}^{(N)}$. The shaded band marks the compactness range of observed neutron stars ($0.1$--$0.25$). Relativistic corrections reduce the critical Rayleigh number by 30--50\% for typical NS compactness, owing to the enhanced effective gravity and pressure--inertia coupling.}}{269}{figure.9.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ for the $l=1$ mode vs.\ compactness $GM/(Rc^{2})$ for three representative nuclear EOSs. Filled circles mark the compactness of a $1.4\,M_{\odot }$ neutron star for each EOS. The horizontal dotted line is the Newtonian value. Softer EOSs (BSk21) produce higher compactness and hence lower critical Rayleigh numbers, implying easier onset of core convection.}}{277}{figure.9.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Critical Rayleigh number $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ vs.\ harmonic degree $l$ for three NS EOS models (APR, SLy, BSk21) and the Newtonian limit. The $l=1$ mode remains the most easily excited in all cases. Higher compactness (BSk21 at $2\,M_{\odot }$) produces the largest reduction. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ra}_{c,\mathrm {rel}}/\mathrm {Ra}_{c,\mathrm {Newton}}$ vs.\ $l$, showing how the relativistic reduction is strongest at low~$l$ and weakens for higher multipoles. The BSk21 model has the smallest ratio ($\sim 0.42$ at $l=1$), corresponding to a critical gradient reduced by nearly 60\% from the Newtonian value.}}{279}{figure.9.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.4}{\ignorespaces Critical Rayleigh number for convection in a spherical shell vs.\ shell thickness ratio $\eta $ for various compactness parameters $\xi $. The Newtonian limit ($\xi = 0$) is shown as a dotted gray line. The shaded region marks the range $\eta \approx 0.85$--$0.95$ typical of neutron star crusts. Relativistic corrections increase $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}$ by factors of 2--3 at typical NS compactness ($\xi \simeq 0.3$--$0.4$), substantially inhibiting convective overturn.}}{290}{figure.9.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.5}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Critical Rayleigh number vs.\ shell ratio $\eta = R_{\mathrm {inner}}/R_{\mathrm {outer}}$ for accreting NS (deep crustal heating, $b \sim r^{-2}$) and cooling NS (uniform neutrino emission, $b \approx 1$), both at compactness $\xi = 0.35$. Newtonian baselines shown for comparison. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ra}_{c}^{\mathrm {accreting}} / \mathrm {Ra}_{c}^{\mathrm {cooling}}$, showing that accreting NSs have systematically higher convective thresholds due to the less efficient coupling of concentrated inner-crust heat sources to buoyancy-driven overturn in the outer shell. The shaded region marks $\eta \approx 0.88$--$0.96$ typical of NS crusts.}}{291}{figure.9.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.6}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Ratio $\Omega _{\mathrm {eff}}/\Omega $ (effective rotation including frame-dragging) at three radial positions within a $1.4\,M_{\odot }$, $R = 10$~km neutron star. Frame-dragging reduces the effective rotation by 15--40\%, with the strongest effect in the deep interior. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}/\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {Newton}}$ (volume-averaged) for several compactness values. The vertical dotted line marks PSR~J1748$-$2446ad, the fastest known pulsar at 716~Hz~(Hessels et al.\ 2006). The Lense--Thirring effect reduces the rotational stabilisation by up to 30\%, lowering the threshold for convective onset.}}{305}{figure.9.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.7}{\ignorespaces \emph {Left:} Lense--Thirring frame-dragging ratio $\omega _{\mathrm {LT}}(R)/\Omega $ at the surface vs.\ spin frequency for five specific pulsars (coloured symbols). Horizontal dashed lines show constant-compactness contours. The frame-dragging fraction depends only on compactness (not spin frequency), but placing pulsars on this diagram illustrates which systems are most affected. \emph {Right:} Ratio $\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {rel}}/\mathrm {Ta}_{\mathrm {Newton}}$ (volume-averaged) for the same pulsars. The fastest and most compact pulsars (J1748$-$2446ad, J1614$-$2230) show the largest reductions ($\sim 20$\%), directly impacting the rotational stabilisation of convection.}}{306}{figure.9.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces Left: relativistic specific angular momentum $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2}$ versus $r/r_{g}$ for Keplerian geodesic flow in Schwarzschild ($a/M=0$) and Kerr ($a/M=0.5,\,0.998$) spacetimes. Filled markers indicate the ISCO for each case. Right: radial derivative $d(\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2})/dr$; the Rayleigh-stable region ($d\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2}/dr>0$) is shaded. The ISCO coincides with $d\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \ell $}\mathaccent "0365{\ell }^{2}/dr=0$ in every case.}}{315}{figure.10.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces Rayleigh criterion near the ISCO for Kerr black holes. Left: specific angular momentum $\ell (r)$ for prograde geodesics with $a/M=0$ (Schwarzschild), $0.5$, $0.9$, and $0.998$. Filled circles mark the ISCO where $d\ell /dr=0$. Right: radial epicyclic frequency $\kappa _{r}^{2}(r)$ for the same four spins. The Rayleigh-unstable region ($\kappa _{r}^{2}<0$, shaded) lies inside the ISCO. As spin increases, the ISCO moves inward and $\kappa _{r}$ develops a sharper maximum, creating a narrow zone for trapped oscillations.}}{316}{figure.10.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.3}{\ignorespaces Relativistic Rayleigh discriminant $\Phi _{\mathrm {rel}}(r)$ for Keplerian circular orbits in Schwarzschild and Kerr spacetimes. The discriminant vanishes at the ISCO (filled circles) and is positive outside. Higher black-hole spin shifts the ISCO inward, extending the stable Keplerian flow region. The shaded area marks the Rayleigh-unstable regime ($\Phi _{\mathrm {rel}}<0$).}}{323}{figure.10.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.4}{\ignorespaces Epicyclic and orbital frequencies versus $r/r_{g}$ for Kerr black holes with $a/M=0$ (Schwarzschild), $0.5$, and $0.998$. Frequencies are shown in Hz for $M=10\,M_{\odot }$. The inverted triangles mark the maximum of $\kappa _{r}$, which sets the highest possible radial QPO frequency. The vertical grey lines indicate the ISCO for each spin.}}{330}{figure.10.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.5}{\ignorespaces Epicyclic frequency predictions compared with observed twin-peak QPOs for two X-ray binaries. Left: GRS~1915+105 ($M=14\,M_{\odot }$, $a/M=0.98$); right: GRO~J1655-40 ($M=6.3\,M_{\odot }$, $a/M=0.70$). Solid red: radial epicyclic frequency $f_{r}$. Dashed blue: vertical epicyclic frequency $f_{\theta }$. Dash-dotted green: orbital frequency $f_{\phi }$. Horizontal shaded bands mark the observed QPO frequencies with their uncertainties. The ISCO is indicated by a vertical dotted line. The agreement between the epicyclic predictions and the observed twin-peak frequencies supports the resonance model of \citet {AbramowiczKluzniak2001}.}}{331}{figure.10.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.6}{\ignorespaces Left: ratio of relativistic to classical Reynolds number versus the correction parameter~$\xi $, with astrophysical regimes indicated. Right: relativistic enhancement of the critical Taylor number (solid) and suppression of the Shakura--Sunyaev $\alpha $-parameter (dashed) as functions of~$\xi $.}}{338}{figure.10.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.7}{\ignorespaces BDNK modifications to the Shakura--Sunyaev $\alpha $-disk for $M=10\,M_{\odot }$, $\alpha _{\mathrm {cl}}=0.1$. Left: relativistic correction parameter $\xi (r)$ for three accretion rates $\dot {M}/\dot {M}_{\mathrm {Edd}}=0.01$, $0.1$, and $0.5$. Vertical dotted lines mark the zone~(a)/(b) and (b)/(c) boundaries. Centre: ratio $\alpha _{\mathrm {rel}}/\alpha _{\mathrm {cl}}=1/(1+\xi )$, showing suppression of the effective viscosity parameter in the inner disk. Right: Reynolds number enhancement $\mathrm {Re}_{\mathrm {rel}}/\mathrm {Re}_{\mathrm {cl}}=(1+\xi )$.}}{340}{figure.10.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.8}{\ignorespaces Left: critical Taylor number $T_{c}^{\mathrm {rel}}$ versus $\mu =\Omega _{2}/\Omega _{1}$ in the narrow-gap limit for several values of the relativistic correction parameter~$\xi $. Right: ratio $T_{c}^{\mathrm {rel}}/T_{c}^{\mathrm {cl}}=(1+\xi )^{2}$ as a function of~$\xi $, with astrophysical regimes marked.}}{347}{figure.10.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.9}{\ignorespaces Left: critical Taylor number versus stability parameter $\kappa $ for the wide-gap case $\eta =0.5$, comparing classical and relativistic predictions for several values of $p/\rho _{0}c^{2}$. Right: relativistic correction $\delta _{\mathrm {rel}}$ to the critical Taylor number as a function of gap ratio~$\eta $. The thick-disk regime ($\eta \lesssim 0.5$, wide gap) shows slightly larger corrections than the thin-disk regime ($\eta \to 1$, narrow gap).}}{353}{figure.10.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.10}{\ignorespaces Thick disk stability around Sgr~A* ($M=4\times 10^{6}\,M_{\odot }$). Left: disk aspect ratio $H/R$ versus $r/r_{g}$ for the ADAF solution (solid, $H/R=c_{s}/v_{K}$) compared with a thin disk (dashed, $H/R=0.05$). The shaded region marks the geometrically thick regime where the wide-gap Couette analysis applies. Right: critical Taylor number $T_{c}$ versus gap ratio $\eta =R_{\mathrm {in}}/R_{\mathrm {out}}$ for classical (solid black) and relativistic (coloured) predictions at several radii. The orange-shaded band indicates the typical $\eta $ range of an ADAF torus. The relativistic correction $\delta _{\mathrm {rel}}$ reduces $T_{c}$ by $3$--$15\%$ in the inner disk, destabilising the torus.}}{355}{figure.10.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.11}{\ignorespaces Dean number vs.\ disk curvature for accretion stream curved channels. Left: critical Reynolds number as a function of $d/R_1$ for several values of $V_m/c$. Right: relativistic Dean parameter $\Lambda _{\mathrm {rel},c}$ vs.\ $V_m/c$. Shaded bands indicate astrophysically relevant regimes.}}{361}{figure.10.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.12}{\ignorespaces Left: relativistic Dean number $De_{\mathrm {rel}}$ (solid) and classical $De$ (dotted) vs.\ mass transfer rate for neutron-star and black-hole X-ray binaries. The horizontal dashed line marks the critical Dean number $De_{c} = 92{,}975$. The blue shaded band indicates the typical LMXB range $\dot {M} \sim 10^{-10}$--$10^{-8}\,M_{\odot }\,\mathrm {yr}^{-1}$. Right: stream velocity $V_{\mathrm {stream}}/c$ vs.\ $\dot {M}$, showing that black-hole systems reach mildly relativistic velocities. The right axis gives the fractional relativistic correction $\sim \tfrac {5}{2}(V/c)^{2}$.}}{362}{figure.10.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.13}{\ignorespaces Stability map for a disk with radial pressure gradient: critical Taylor number vs.\ $\lambda $ for several values of $\Xi $. The relativistic correction $(1+\Xi )^2$ uniformly raises the stability boundary.}}{369}{figure.10.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.14}{\ignorespaces Stability diagram for astrophysical jets with axial flow plus rotation. Left: $\Psi _{\mathrm {rel}}(r)$ for pure axial flow. Right: $\Phi _{\mathrm {rel}}(r)$ for combined rotation and axial flow. Negative values indicate instability.}}{377}{figure.10.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.15}{\ignorespaces Viscous spiral flow in accretion: Re--Ta parameter space. Left: stability boundaries for several BDNK parameters $\mathcal {T}$. Right: critical Taylor number vs.\ $\mathcal {T}$ for fixed Re. The shaded band indicates relativistic accretion column conditions.}}{384}{figure.10.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.16}{\ignorespaces MRI dispersion in a Keplerian disk: growth rate $\sigma /\Omega $ vs.\ wavenumber for several $v_A/c$. Left: classical. Right: relativistic (bounded $v_A$).}}{394}{figure.10.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.17}{\ignorespaces Left: ratio $\lambda _{\mathrm {MRI}}/H$ vs.\ $r/r_{g}$ for the Sgr~A$^{*}$ ADAF model with $M = 4\times 10^{6}\,M_{\odot }$, $\dot {M} = 10^{-8}\,M_{\odot }\,\mathrm {yr}^{-1}$. The MRI is resolved ($\lambda _{\mathrm {MRI}} < H$) in the green shaded region. Right: disk aspect ratio $H/r$ and Alfv\'en speed $v_{A}/c$ vs.\ radius. Dashed lines show $v_{A}/c$ for three values of $\beta _{\mathrm {pl}}$. In the strongly magnetised case ($\beta _{\mathrm {pl}} = 1$), $v_{A}/c$ can reach $\sim 10^{-2}$ near the ISCO, making the relativistic Alfv\'en bound~\eqref {eq:rel-9-vA} relevant.}}{395}{figure.10.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.18}{\ignorespaces Kink modes in NS magnetospheres. Left: growth rate vs.\ wavenumber for several $\Xi $. Right: critical axial field for stabilisation in the $(\Xi , v_A/c)$ plane.}}{404}{figure.10.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.19}{\ignorespaces Dissipative MRI: critical $\mathscr {T}_c(Q)$ for conducting and non-conducting boundaries. Left: co-rotating ($\mu > 0$). Right: counter-rotating ($\mu = -1$). Solid: classical; dashed: relativistic ($v_A/c = 0.1$).}}{415}{figure.10.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.20}{\ignorespaces Left: MRI growth rate $\sigma /\Omega $ vs.\ magnetic Reynolds number $Rm$ for several normalised wavenumbers $k_{z}v_{A}/\Omega $. The ideal limit $\sigma _{\max } = 3\Omega /4$ is approached for $Rm \gg 1$. The red shaded region marks the dead zone ($Rm \lesssim 1$). Right: comparison of BDNK (dashed) and classical (solid) growth rates at the optimal wavenumber, for $v_{A}/c = 0.01$, $0.1$, and $0.3$. The BDNK corrections widen the dead zone boundary (orange band) relative to the classical prediction. These shifts are quantified by equation~\eqref {eq:rel-9-Rm-crit-rel} and are of order $v_{A}^{2}/c^{2}$.}}{416}{figure.10.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.21}{\ignorespaces GRMHD MRI near the ISCO. Left: maximum growth rate vs.\ radius for Kerr black holes with various spin parameters; dotted lines mark the ISCO. Right: effective $\alpha $-parameter from MRI-driven turbulence compared with GRMHD simulation data.}}{421}{figure.10.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.22}{\ignorespaces Left: normalised maximum MRI growth rate $\sigma _{\max }/\Omega _{K}$ vs.\ $r/r_{g}$ for Kerr black holes with $a_{*} = 0$, $0.5$, $0.9$, and $0.998$. Dotted vertical lines mark the ISCO for each spin. The dashed horizontal line is the ideal Keplerian value $3/4$. Near the ISCO, $\sigma _{\max }$ can exceed $3\Omega _{K}/4$ because $\kappa ^{2}\to 0$. Right: effect of bounded Alfv\'en speed at $a_{*} = 0.9$; for $v_{A}/c = 0.3$ the growth rate is reduced by $\sim 5\%$, consistent with the relativistic correction $\sim v_{A}^{2}/(2c^{2})$. The profile connects smoothly to the results of Liska et al.~\cite {Liska2021} on large-scale poloidal field dynamo action in GRMHD simulations of magnetised accretion flows.}}{422}{figure.10.22}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces Shock-driven Rayleigh--Taylor instability in core-collapse supernovae. \textit {Left:} Growth rate $\sigma $ as a function of the density ratio $\rho _{2}/\rho _{1}$ across the composition interface, for classical ($\xi =0$) and several relativistic values of $\xi = p/(\rho c^{2})$. \textit {Right:} Classical vs.\ relativistic Atwood number. Parameters correspond to a proto-neutron star of mass $1.4\,M_{\odot }$ and shock radius $150\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {km}$.}}{434}{figure.11.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces Deep calculation: RT instability in core-collapse supernovae with nuclear EOS. \textit {(a)}~Growth rate $\sigma (k)$ for $R_{\mathrm {shock}}=200\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {km}$, $\rho _{2}/\rho _{1}=7$, and several values of the nuclear EOS parameter~$\xi $. \textit {(b)}~Relativistic Atwood number vs.\ $\xi $ for different compression ratios; the shaded band indicates the CCSN post-shock regime. \textit {(c)}~Growth timescale vs.\ spherical harmonic order $\ell $, compared with the convective overturn ($\tau _{\mathrm {conv}}$) and advection ($\tau _{\mathrm {adv}}$) timescales. \textit {(d)}~Compressible corrections (Duffell 2011) and Richtmyer--Meshkov coupling (Matsumoto et~al.\ 2021).}}{435}{figure.11.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.3}{\ignorespaces Variational bounds on the RT growth rate for an exponentially stratified relativistic fluid. \textit {Left:} Exact growth rates for modes $m = 1, 2, 3$ (solid) compared with the upper bound from a parabolic trial function (dotted). The asymptotic limit $n_{\max } = \sqrt {g\beta }$ is shown as a dashed line. \textit {Right:} Maximum growth rate ($m=1$) for different values of the relativistic parameter~$\xi $, showing the systematic reduction of the bound due to enhanced inertia.}}{442}{figure.11.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.4}{\ignorespaces Viscous RT instability at the contact interface in binary neutron star mergers. \textit {Left:} Normalised growth rate $\sigma /\sqrt {g/L}$ vs.\ effective Reynolds number for several relativistic Atwood numbers. \textit {Right:} Damping timescale ratio $\tau _{\mathrm {damp}}/\tau _{\mathrm {inviscid}}$ comparing the BDNK first-order theory (solid) with the Israel--Stewart second-order theory (dashed, $\tau _{\pi }/\tau _{0} = 0.1$).}}{449}{figure.11.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.5}{\ignorespaces Deep calculation: Viscous RT instability in NS merger ejecta with BDNK viscosity. \textit {(a)}~RT mixing length vs.\ time post-merger for different nuclear shear viscosities. \textit {(b)}~Fastest-growing wavelength $\lambda _{\max }$ vs.\ time. \textit {(c)}~Viscous RT dispersion relation $\sigma (k)$ at $t = 1\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {ms}$ for different viscosities. \textit {(d)}~BDNK vs.\ Israel--Stewart comparison: the IS theory with relaxation times $\tau _{\pi }$ introduces spurious growth enhancement at high~$k$.}}{450}{figure.11.5}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces Centrifugal stabilisation of RT instability in a relativistic accretion disk. \textit {Left:} Growth rate $n$ vs.\ wavenumber $k$ for increasing angular velocity $\Omega _{\mathrm {rel}}$. \textit {Right:} Ratio $n(\Omega )/n(0)$ as a function of co-rotation speed $\Omega R/c$ for several wavenumbers, comparing Newtonian (dashed) and relativistic (solid) predictions. The relativistic Coriolis enhancement $\Gamma _{\Omega }^{2}$ provides significantly stronger stabilisation at high rotation rates.}}{459}{figure.12.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.1}{\ignorespaces Magnetic stabilisation of RT instability in relativistic jets. \textit {Left:} Critical magnetic field strength for complete stabilisation vs.\ enthalpy ratio, for different Alfv\'en speeds. The relativistic correction factor $\sqrt {1 - v_{A}^{2}/c^{2}}$ reduces the threshold. \textit {Right:} Growth rate vs.\ wavenumber for a vertical magnetic field with $v_{A}/c = 0.5$. The growth rate saturates at $k \to \infty $ (dotted lines), unlike the unbounded $\sqrt {gk}$ behaviour of the unmagnetised case (dashed).}}{469}{figure.13.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.2}{\ignorespaces Deep calculation: Magnetic RT stabilisation in relativistic jets. \textit {(a)}~Critical magnetisation $\sigma _{B,\mathrm {crit}}$ vs.\ jet Lorentz factor for different density ratios; the AGN and GRB regimes are shaded. \textit {(b)}~Critical Alfv\'en speed $v_{A}/c$ at the stabilisation threshold, showing that causality ($v_{A}<c$) is never violated. \textit {(c)}~Growth rate vs.\ wavenumber for a horizontal field at $\Gamma = 10$ with different magnetisations. \textit {(d)}~Vertical-field growth rate saturation for different $\Gamma $ at fixed $\sigma _{B}=0.3$.}}{470}{figure.13.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.3}{\ignorespaces Neutron star oscillation modes. \textit {Left:} Relativistic correction factor $\sigma _{\mathrm {rel}}/\sigma _{\mathrm {Newt}}$ for Kelvin modes $\ell = 2$--$5$ as a function of compactness $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$. The shaded band indicates the typical NS compactness range. \textit {Right:} Physical $f$-mode and $g$-mode frequencies for $\ell = 2$ as a function of NS mass, comparing relativistic (solid) and Newtonian (dashed) predictions.}}{480}{figure.13.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.4}{\ignorespaces Deep calculation: NS oscillation modes and GW detectability. \textit {(a)}~$f$-mode and $g$-mode frequencies vs.\ NS mass for three EOSs (APR4, SLy4, H4). \textit {(b)}~BDNK viscous damping timescale vs.\ mass; dashed curves show the Israel--Stewart prediction with $\tau _{\pi } = 10^{-4}\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {s}$. \textit {(c)}~GW strain vs.\ frequency, overlaid on LIGO~O4, LIGO~A+, and Einstein Telescope sensitivity curves; markers show $f$-mode and $g$-mode signals at $10$ and $50\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {Mpc}$. \textit {(d)}~Relativistic corrections to mode frequency and damping timescale vs.\ compactness for $\ell = 2$, $3$, $4$.}}{481}{figure.13.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Critical KH velocity (normalised to $\sqrt {g/k}$) vs.\ jet Lorentz factor for density ratios $\eta = \hat {\rho }_{\mathrm {amb}}/\hat {\rho }_{\mathrm {jet}}$ ranging from 0.01 (light jet) to 1.0 (matched). \textbf {Right:} Lab-frame growth rate suppression $\sigma _{\mathrm {lab}}/\sigma _0 \propto \Gamma ^{-2}$, with positions of M87, 3C\,273, and 3C\,279 marked. }}{490}{figure.14.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} KH growth rate $\sigma R_{\mathrm {jet}}/c$ vs.\ normalised wavenumber for the M87 jet, for $\Gamma = 3$, 4.5, and~6, with $\eta = 0.01$ and $c_s = c/\sqrt {3}$. Solid: helical ($m=1$); dashed: pinch ($m=0$). The vertical dotted line marks the observed helical wavelength $\lambda \approx 1\,$kpc \citep {WalkerEtAl2018}. \textbf {Right:} KH growth length vs.\ perturbation wavelength; the shaded bands indicate the jet disruption scale and the observed M87 jet length. The star marks the 3C\,84 vortex scale from \cite {Fuentes2025}. }}{491}{figure.14.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic Richardson number $J_{\mathrm {rel}}(z)$ across a $\tanh $ shear layer for $\Gamma _0 = 1$ (classical), 2, 5, and 10. The dashed line marks the Miles--Howard threshold $J_{\mathrm {rel}} = 1/4$. \textbf {Right:} Critical shear-layer width (normalised) vs.\ $\Gamma $ for stability, with observed positions of M87 and Cen~A marked. }}{502}{figure.15.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} KH growth rate $\sigma R_{\mathrm {jet}}/c$ vs.\ normalised wavenumber for a spine-sheath jet model ($\Gamma _{s}=10$, $\Gamma _{\mathrm {sh}}=2$, $\eta _s=10^{-3}$, $\eta _{\mathrm {sh}}=0.05$) compared with a uniform jet ($\Gamma =6$, $\eta =0.01$). The red curve shows the coupled growth rate including mode interaction at the two interfaces. \textbf {Right:} Ratio of spine-sheath to uniform growth rates, with an inset showing the jet cross-section schematic. Parameters from \cite {Hardee2007, MizunoHardeeNishikawa2007}. }}{504}{figure.15.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Lab-frame KH growth rate (normalised to $k\,c$) vs.\ Lorentz factor for hot ($c_{s}=c/\sqrt {3}$) and cold ($c_{s}=0.1\,c$) jets, including Gaussian structured profiles with $\Gamma _{\mathrm {core}} = 100$ and 300. The shaded band marks the GRB regime ($\Gamma \sim 100$--$1000$). \textbf {Right:} Stability map in the $(\Gamma ,\,c_{s}/c)$ plane; the colour scale shows $\log _{10}(\sigma _{\mathrm {lab}}/kc)$, with dashed lines marking the compressible stabilisation boundary for $c_{s}=c/\sqrt {3}$ and $c_{s}=0.1\,c$. }}{513}{figure.16.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} KH stability map in the $(\Gamma , \eta )$ plane, where $\eta = \hat {\rho }_{\mathrm {jet}}/\hat {\rho }_{\mathrm {amb}}$. The colour scale shows the normalised growth rate; the white dashed contour marks the Miles--Howard boundary $\mathrm {Ri}_{\mathrm {rel}} = 1/4$. Symbols mark 3C\,84 \citep {Fuentes2025}, M87 \citep {WalkerEtAl2018}, and Cen~A. \textbf {Right:} Richardson number profile across the 3C\,84 shear layer ($\Gamma = 2.5$); the red shaded region marks the KH-unstable zone ($\mathrm {Ri}_{\mathrm {rel}} < 1/4$). The purple band indicates the observed vortex location. }}{515}{figure.16.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Critical magnetisation $\sigma _{\mathrm {crit}}$ for complete KH stabilisation vs.\ jet Lorentz factor, for density ratios $\eta = 0.01$--$1.0$. \textbf {Right:} Normalised KH growth rate vs.\ magnetisation parameter $\sigma $ for $\Gamma = 3$, 10, and 30 (with $\eta = 0.1$). The vertical dashed lines mark the critical $\sigma $ for each $\Gamma $. }}{522}{figure.16.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} KH growth rate downstream of a recollimation shock ($\Gamma _{\mathrm {pre}} = 10$, $\sigma _{\mathrm {pre}} = 0.1$, compression ratio $\mathcal {C} = 3$). Solid red: MHD (with decaying field); dashed blue: hydrodynamic (no field). The magnetisation $\sigma _{B}(z)$ and critical magnetisation $\sigma _{\mathrm {crit}}(z)$ are shown on the right axis. The vertical dotted line marks the KH onset distance. \textbf {Right:} Critical magnetisation boundary downstream of the shock for different pre-shock Lorentz factors ($\Gamma _{\mathrm {pre}} = 5$, 10, 20); the dashed black curve shows the actual $\sigma _{B}(z)$ trajectory. The star marks the simulation result of \cite {MukherjeeBodo2025}. }}{524}{figure.16.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic dispersion relation for the varicose mode of an infinite self-gravitating cylinder at several values of the compactness $\mathcal {C}$. The critical wavenumber $x_{a}=1.0668$ is unchanged, but the peak shifts to smaller $x$ (longer wavelengths) as $\mathcal {C}$ increases. \textbf {Right:} Ratio of the relativistic to Newtonian most-unstable fragmentation wavelength as a function of $\mathcal {C}$, with astrophysical regimes indicated.}}{532}{figure.17.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Jeans length (normalized to filament radius) versus redshift for a model dark-matter filament with overdensity $\delta =10$ and cold gas fraction $\xi _{\mathrm {gas}}=10^{-5}$. The Newtonian gravitational fragmentation scale (blue) and its relativistic correction (red) nearly coincide at $z\lesssim 100$; the thermal Jeans length (green, dashed) is shown for comparison. \textbf {Right:} Ratio $\lambda _{J}^{\mathrm {rel}}/\lambda _{J}^{\mathrm {N}}$ and compactness parameter $\mathcal {C}$ as functions of redshift. At $z\sim 1000$ (recombination) the relativistic correction reaches the percent level.}}{533}{figure.17.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Dispersion relation for the gravitational instability of a magnetized relativistic cylinder at different axial field strengths $H/H_{0,\mathrm {rel}}$. The magnetic field raises the critical wavenumber (shorter minimum unstable wavelength) and reduces the peak growth rate. \textbf {Right:} Minimum unstable wavelength (solid, left axis) and maximum growth rate (dashed, right axis) as functions of $H/H_{0,\mathrm {rel}}$, with astrophysical jet regimes indicated.}}{546}{figure.17.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Observed versus predicted knot spacing for five AGN jets (M87, 3C~273, Cygnus~A, Centaurus~A, 3C~31). The predicted spacing is computed from the magnetic-stabilization dispersion relation~\eqref {eq:rel-12-110-17} using the relativistic gravitating density $\rho _{G}$ and enthalpy $w_{\mathrm {tot}}$. The dashed line marks perfect agreement; the grey band a factor-of-three range. \textbf {Right:} Theoretical knot spacing $\lambda _{\mathrm {knot}}/R_{\mathrm {jet}}$ versus magnetic field strength $H/H_{0,\mathrm {rel}}$, with observed data points overlaid. The Newtonian ($H=0$) fragmentation scale $\lambda /R\approx 10.8$ is shown for reference.}}{547}{figure.17.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.5}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic suppression of the capillary growth rate and enhancement of the breakup timescale as functions of the enthalpy ratio $w/\rho = (\varepsilon +p)/(\rho c^{2})$, with astrophysical regimes indicated. \textbf {Right:} Observer-frame breakup timescale for a GRB jet (cocoon-confined, $R=10^{7}\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {cm}$) as a function of bulk Lorentz factor $\gamma $, for several internal enthalpy ratios. The grey band marks the observed GRB variability range.}}{559}{figure.17.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.6}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Observer-frame capillary breakup timescale versus bulk Lorentz factor $\gamma _{\mathrm {jet}}$ for a GRB jet with $R_{\mathrm {jet}}=10^{7}\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {cm}$ and cocoon pressure $p_{\mathrm {cocoon}}=10^{18}\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {erg\,cm^{-3}}$. Curves correspond to different enthalpy ratios; the grey band marks the observed GRB variability range ($10$--$100\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {ms}$). Data points indicate observed variability timescales and inferred Lorentz factors for individual GRBs. \textbf {Right:} Relativistic suppression of the capillary growth rate $\sigma _{\max }^{\mathrm {rel}}/\sigma _{\max }^{\mathrm {N}}$ and corresponding enhancement of the breakup timescale versus enthalpy ratio $w/\rho c^{2}$.}}{560}{figure.17.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.7}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Kink growth length $L_{\mathrm {kink}}/R_{\mathrm {jet}}$ versus toroidal-to-axial magnetic field ratio $B_{\varphi }/B_{z}$ for several values of the bulk Lorentz factor $\gamma $. Horizontal bands indicate the scales at which FR~I jets disrupt ($10$--$100\,R_{\mathrm {jet}}$) and FR~II jets remain coherent ($>500\,R_{\mathrm {jet}}$). \textbf {Right:} Morphology diagram in the $(\gamma ,\,B_{\varphi }/B_{z})$ plane. Contours show constant $L_{\mathrm {kink}}/R_{\mathrm {jet}}$. Observed AGN jets are overlaid, with FR~I sources occupying the short-growth-length (disrupted) region and FR~II sources the long-growth-length (coherent) region.}}{570}{figure.17.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.8}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Relativistic Kruskal--Shafranov critical ratio $B_{z}/B_{\varphi }$ for the $m=-1$ kink mode as a function of bulk Lorentz factor $\gamma $, for several axial wavenumbers $kR_{1}$. Dotted lines show the classical (static) values. Relativistic bulk flow strongly suppresses the required stabilising field. \textbf {Right:} Application to magnetar giant flares: the dipolar $B_{z}/B_{\varphi }$ ratio (solid) decreases with distance from the neutron star, crossing the kink-stability threshold (dashed horizontal lines) at radii that depend on the fireball Lorentz factor $\gamma $.}}{571}{figure.17.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.9}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Critical adiabatic index $\gamma _{c}$ versus stellar compactness $\mathcal {C} = GM/(Rc^{2})$ for three structure-dependent values of $\kappa $ (eq.~\ref {eq:rel-13-gamma-c}). The shaded bands mark the compactness ranges of white dwarfs ($\mathcal {C} \sim 10^{-4}$--$10^{-3}$) and neutron stars ($\mathcal {C} \sim 0.1$--$0.35$). Supermassive stars (SMS) with radiation-dominated interiors ($\gamma _{\mathrm {eff}} \approx 4/3$) are driven unstable by even a tiny GR correction. \textbf {Right:} Normalised oscillation frequency $\omega ^{2}$ versus $\gamma $ for several compactness values, showing how the stability boundary shifts rightward as $\mathcal {C}$ increases. Configurations in the red-shaded region are dynamically unstable. }}{580}{figure.17.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.10}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Maximum white-dwarf mass as a function of central density $\rho _{c}$. The Newtonian curve (blue) asymptotes to $M_{\mathrm {Ch}} \approx 1.44\,M_{\odot }$; the GR-corrected curve (red) turns over at $M_{\max ,\mathrm {GR}} < M_{\mathrm {Ch}}$ and the configuration becomes unstable (red shading) at high density. The vertical dashed line marks the NR/UR transition density $\rho _{\mathrm {rel}}$. \textbf {Right:} Effective adiabatic index $\gamma _{\mathrm {eff}}$ (blue) and GR critical index $\gamma _{c} = 4/3 + (38/21)\,GM/(Rc^{2})$ (red) as functions of $M/M_{\mathrm {Ch}}$. The crossing defines the GR instability window (red shading). }}{582}{figure.17.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.11}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Ratio of relativistic to classical Jeans mass as a function of the equation-of-state parameter $w = p/\varepsilon $. The relativistic Jeans mass is always \emph {smaller} than the classical value, reflecting the pressure-destabilisation effect. The radiation-dominated case ($w = 1/3$) and the causal limit ($w = 1$) are marked. \textbf {Right:} Enhancement of the Jeans wavenumber $k_{J,\mathrm {rel}}/k_{J,\mathrm {classical}}$, showing that GR renders shorter-wavelength modes gravitationally unstable. The shaded blue region indicates the additional $k$-space made unstable by relativistic effects. }}{588}{figure.17.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.12}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Cosmological Jeans mass $M_{J}$ versus $1+z$, comparing Newtonian (blue) and relativistic (red) treatments. Key epochs are marked: matter--radiation equality $z_{\mathrm {eq}}$, recombination $z_{\mathrm {rec}}$, and photon decoupling $z_{\mathrm {dec}}$. The dramatic drop at decoupling enables baryonic structure formation. Era labels indicate radiation-dominated, matter-dominated, and dark-energy epochs. \textbf {Right:} Ratio $M_{J,\mathrm {rel}}/M_{J,\mathrm {Newton}}$ (red, left axis) and sound speed $c_{s}/c$ (green, right axis). The relativistic correction is maximal ($\sim 30$--$60\%$) during the radiation-dominated era and negligible after decoupling. }}{590}{figure.17.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.13}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Toomre $Q$ parameter as a function of galactocentric radius for a model spiral galaxy with a flat rotation curve ($V_{c} = 220$~km/s) and exponential surface density profile. The thermal-only case ($c_{s} = 10$~km/s) is compared with the magnetically supported case ($B = 5\,\mu $G). Orange bands mark schematic spiral-arm locations where enhanced surface density drives $Q$ down toward the instability threshold $Q = 1$. \textbf {Right:} Normalised relativistic $Q_{\mathrm {rel}}$ as a function of $c_{s}/c$ for different magnetic field strengths in a compact disk, showing how relativistic effects (enthalpy corrections, bounded $v_{A}$) modify the stability boundary at high sound speeds. }}{596}{figure.17.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.14}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Toomre parameter $Q$ versus galactocentric radius for the Milky Way. Individual components: stellar (dashed orange), gas (dashed green), effective two-fluid $Q_{\mathrm {eff}}$ (solid red), and $Q_{\mathrm {eff}}$ without the dark matter halo (dotted purple). The horizontal black line marks $Q = 1$ (instability threshold); the solar position $R_{\odot } = 8\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {kpc}$ is indicated. \textbf {Right:} $Q_{\mathrm {eff}}$ in the inter-arm disk (blue) and within spiral arms (dashed red, assuming $\times 2$ overdensity). Orange bands mark schematic spiral-arm crossings. In the inner disk ($R \lesssim 5\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\mathrm {kpc}$), spiral-arm compression drives $Q < 1$, triggering star formation. }}{599}{figure.17.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.1}{\ignorespaces Stability map in the $(\beta , q)$ plane from the MHD energy principle $\delta ^{2}W$. Red/yellow regions are unstable ($\delta ^{2}W < 0$); green/blue regions are stable ($\delta ^{2}W > 0$). The solid black curve marks the marginal stability boundary $\delta ^{2}W = 0$. \textbf {Left:} Classical (Newtonian) result. The $q = 1$ boundary (white dashed) corresponds to the $m = 1$ kink instability; the diagonal boundary reflects the ballooning $\beta $-limit. \textbf {Right:} Relativistic case with $v_{A}/c = 0.3$ (representative of neutron-star magnetospheres or hot accretion flows). The dashed white curve reproduces the classical boundary for comparison. Relativistic corrections---enhanced enthalpy inertia and bounded Alfv\'en speed---shrink the stable region, lowering the critical $\beta $-limit. }}{608}{figure.18.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Neutron star mass--radius curves for four representative equations of state. Circles mark the maximum mass (stability boundary) for each EOS. Observed NS measurements (purple error ellipses) are overlaid. The dashed line marks the causality limit $c_{s} = c$; the black shaded region is inside the Schwarzschild radius. \textbf {Right:} Contour map of $\delta ^{2}W$ (normalised) in the $M$--$R$ plane. Red/yellow: unstable ($\delta ^{2}W < 0$); green/blue: stable ($\delta ^{2}W > 0$). The solid black curve is the marginal stability boundary $\delta ^{2}W = 0$. White curves are EOS tracks; white stars mark observed NSs. The BDNK viscous correction modifies damping rates but leaves the black boundary unchanged. }}{610}{figure.18.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Left:} Normalised Parker instability growth rate $\sigma ^{2}/(g/H)$ as a function of normalised horizontal wavenumber $k_{x}H$ for several values of the plasma $\beta = 8\pi p/B^{2}$. Solid curves are the classical result; dashed curves show the relativistic case with $c_{s}/c = 0.3$. Relativistic effects enhance the peak growth rate through the factor $(1 + 3c_{s}^{2}/c^{2})/2$ in the gravitational source. \textbf {Right:} Ratio of relativistic to classical critical wavelength (blue, left axis) and peak growth rate (red, right axis) as functions of $c_{s}/c$ for $\beta = 1$. The critical wavelength \emph {decreases} (shorter wavelengths become unstable) while the growth rate \emph {increases} with increasing relativistic parameter. }}{616}{figure.18.3}%
